    "id": "train-6300",
    "question": "Bennett sells window screens. He sold twice as many window screens in February as he sold last month. In February, Bennett sold a fourth of what he sold in March. If Bennet sold 8800 window screens in March, how many screens did Bennett sell from January to March?",
    "gold": "12100",
    "answer": "In February, Bennett sold 8800/4 = <<8800/4=2200>>2200 window screens.\nIn January, Bennett sold 2200/2 = <<2200/2=1100>>1100 window screens.\nBetween January and March, Bennett sold 8800+2200+1100 = <<8800+2200+1100=12100>>12100 window screens.\n#### 12100"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine the total number of window screens Bennett sold from January to March, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the number of window screens sold in March.\n2. Use the information about February to find the number sold in February.\n3. Use the information about February to find the number sold in January.\n\nGiven:\n- Bennett sold 8800 window screens in March.\n- In February, Bennett sold a fourth of what he sold in March.\n- In February, Bennett sold twice as many window screens as he sold in January.\n\nFirst, calculate the number of window screens sold in February:\n\\[\n\\text{February sales} = \\frac{1}{4} \\times \\text{March sales} = \\frac{1}{4} \\times 8800 = 2200\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate the number of window screens sold in January:\n\\[\n\\text{January sales} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{February sales} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 2200 = 1100\n\\]\n\nNow, sum the sales from January, February, and March to find the total number of window screens sold from January to March:\n\\[\n\\text{Total sales} = \\text{January sales} + \\text{February sales} + \\text{March sales} = 1100 + 2200 + 8800 = 12100\n\\]\n\nThus, the total number of window screens Bennett sold from January to March is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{12100}\n\\]",
